<section class="section">
  <div class="container prose" style="max-width:980px">

    <h2 style="margin-top:0">The discount, in plain numbers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Month one:</strong> $175, the standard rate. The first payment is what kicks off the build, same as for every client.</li>
      <li><strong>Month two:</strong> $0, on the house. The discount lands as a one-month free credit applied to your second invoice automatically. You see "First responders credit applied" on the invoice line and a $0 total.</li>
      <li><strong>Month three onward:</strong> back to the standard $175. Same plan, same scope, same direct support.</li>
      <li><strong>Total savings:</strong> $175. One month free, no recurring rate change, no expiration logic to track.</li>
      <li><strong>Free 5-point audit either way.</strong> If you are not yet ready to build, the <a href="/audit/" class="inline-link">free audit</a> is also free, and I will flag you for the credit the day you do hire me.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;background:var(--bg-alt);border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.65"><strong>Why "month two free" instead of an ongoing percentage off?</strong> Two reasons. (1) It is simpler to track on both ends &mdash; one credit, one invoice, done. (2) The thank-you is concentrated rather than diluted. A one-month credit feels like an actual gift; a few dollars off every invoice tends to disappear into the noise of monthly billing.</p>

    <h2>Who qualifies</h2>
    <p>Anyone with verifiable service in any of the following roles, current or retired, plus their immediate family:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sworn law enforcement.</strong> Municipal, county, state, federal. Includes corrections officers, parole officers, and sheriff's deputies.</li>
      <li><strong>Firefighters.</strong> Career, volunteer, wildland, and structural. Includes federal/state agency wildland crews and rural fire-protection-district volunteers.</li>
      <li><strong>EMS.</strong> EMTs, paramedics, advanced practice paramedics, flight paramedics, ambulance crew.</li>
      <li><strong>911 telecommunicators.</strong> Public-safety answering point dispatchers and call-takers.</li>
      <li><strong>Search and rescue.</strong> Sworn or volunteer SAR personnel attached to a recognized organization.</li>
      <li><strong>Federal / Canadian equivalents.</strong> RCMP, provincial / territorial police, OPP, S&ucirc;reté du Québec, Canadian fire services, Canadian EMS.</li>
      <li><strong>Spouses, children, parents, and siblings</strong> of any qualifying responder, same credit, same verification (one shared photo of the responder's documentation, plus a one-line note identifying the relationship).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>How verification works:</strong> One photo or scan of your department / agency ID, badge with department name, or a recent paystub from a qualifying agency (with dollar amounts redacted is fine). For volunteer roles, a letter from your chief / commander on department letterhead works. Email it once when we start; I delete the file the moment I confirm. I do not retain copies.</p>

    <h2>How to claim it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Book a 20-minute discovery call.</strong> <a href="/contact/" class="inline-link">Standard contact form</a>. In the message, mention you are claiming the first responders credit.</li>
      <li><strong>On the call, we figure out the build.</strong> Same conversation I have with anyone, what your business needs, what kind of site fits, which plan makes sense.</li>
      <li><strong>Send proof of service.</strong> One photo of your department ID or a single paystub, by email. Takes 30 seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>I tag your account for the credit.</strong> Your first invoice is the standard $175. Your second invoice has the credit applied automatically and totals $0. From month three onward, billing is the standard rate.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why I do this</h2>
    <p>A meaningful number of first responders run a side or post-retirement service business of one kind or another, whether that is off-duty contracting, security consulting, training services, fire-watch, building inspections, or an EMS-adjacent business. The schedule is unforgiving, and the work is harder than the public tends to realize. A free month is the difference, for some owners, between testing the engagement risk-free and not testing it at all.</p>

